  <section class="py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 lg:px-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Quick answers to the questions we hear most.</p>
      <dl class="mt-8 space-y-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">What is acupressure? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Acupressure is a self-administered technique from Traditional Chinese Medicine. You apply firm finger pressure to specific points on the body — called acupoints — that lie along energy channels known as meridians. Unlike acupuncture, nothing pierces the skin, which makes it safe and easy to learn at home.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How does acupressure work? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">In TCM, pressing points clears blocked energy (qi) so it flows smoothly along the meridians. From a modern view, stimulating points activates the body's own pain-modulating pathways, releases tension in surrounding muscle, and shifts the nervous system toward its rest-and-digest state — which helps explain the effects on pain, nausea and anxiety.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Does acupressure actually work? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">The evidence is strongest for nausea and vomiting (a Cochrane review of P6 stimulation for post-operative nausea and vomiting), pain, and anxiety. Meta-analyses also support it for sleep and headaches. Studies find it does not work equally for everything — evidence for some conditions is still preliminary — and it should complement, not replace, medical care.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">What are the best acupressure points for headaches? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">LI4 (Hegu) on the hand and GB20 (Fengchi) at the base of the skull are the two most-studied points for headache relief. Taiyang at the temples helps side-of-head pain, and PC6 is useful when a migraine brings nausea. A 2024 systematic review and meta-analysis of 14 randomized controlled trials found acupressure associated with significant reductions in headache intensity and frequency compared with control, though the review noted the supporting trials were small and heterogeneous.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is acupressure safe? Who should avoid it? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">In controlled trials no serious adverse events were reported. Avoid pressure over open wounds, burns, varicose veins, surgical sites and infection. In pregnancy, stay away from LI4, SP6, BL60 and GB21. Use light pressure if you take blood thinners, and check with a doctor if you have a clotting disorder or recent surgery.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">What is the difference between acupressure and acupuncture? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Both target the same acupoints and meridians. Acupuncture uses thin needles inserted by a licensed practitioner, and its effects are often described as stronger and longer lasting. Acupressure uses only finger pressure on the surface, so you can practice it on yourself any time — both are complementary tools.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press an acupressure point? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Most guides recommend firm, steady pressure for 45-90 seconds per point, using small circular motions, repeated 1-3 times on both sides where applicable. You should feel a dull ache or warmth — never sharp pain or bruising. Deep breathing through each hold improves the effect.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between px-5 py-4 font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can acupressure help with anxiety and sleep? <span class="ml-2 shrink-0 text-stone-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span></summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-4 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">The evidence is promising but not definitive. A systematic review and meta-analysis of acupressure for anxiety found a positive overall direction of effect, and a randomized trial found acupressure improved sleep quality in nursing-home residents. Yintang, HT7, SP6 and KI1 are the points most used in these studies.</div>
        </details>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

